despite rising covet 19 infections and  the risk of violence  tens of thousands of colombians took to  the streets for a fourth straight day on  saturday  in bogota one group gathered at the  national park to continue  protesting against a controversial tax  reform that they say would raise taxes  on the middle class  at a time of great economic pain  among them 28 year old monica paredes  who lost their job during the pandemic  i still haven't been able to pay my  nine-year-old son's school tuition  because last year has been horrible for  me we are tired of not having a decent  health system  of not having institutions that work in  our favor  rising poverty and unemployment  following covet 19 restrictions have  people increasingly venting their anger  in the streets  openly defying official pleas to stay  home  it's easy to stay home when you're not  hungry but poor head of household  women don't have that luxury and have to  sell informally on the streets to  survive  that's why we're marching to tell this  government we are hungry and we need  help  isolated looting vandalism and clashes  mark the previous days  especially in colombia's third largest  city kali  where at least three people have died  allegedly shot by the police  but human rights organizations say the  real death toll could be much higher  on saturday most of the day was peaceful  until the police stopped a group of  demonstrators  the protesters are now trying to reach  the private home of president ivan duque  which is roughly  a couple of blocks up here there's a  huge police presence  in front of his condominium and as you  can see here the police  and anti-riot gear is stopping the  protesters from moving forward  as protesters tried to move on the  police shot tear gas  and stun grenades protesters responded  herding rocks and burning barricades  the chaos has forced president ivan  duque to backpedal on the tax reform  plans  promising to rewrite the bill excluding  taxes on basic food items  utilities and gasoline that angered  protesters  but some analysts believe it will not be  enough  i think that the government hasn't  understood that the political class is  not discussing that they think that just  withdrawing the tax reform is going to  be enough to appease the protest  uh but i think that we're past that  point and and  basically the sort of conversation that  we need from this moment  on has to deal with many more issues  than the tax reform  once again clashes continued into the  evening  signaling that protesters will not  easily stop  alessandra al jazeera bogota
 

